</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we address and investigate two main challenges when detecting conceptually equivalent questions in longitudinal surveys:
(1) The linguistic challenge of capturing semantic equivalence despite surface-level variations in vocabulary and syntax. For instance, “dwelling” may evolve to “household” over time while maintaining the same underlying concept.
(2) The structural challenge in which equivalent concepts may be distributed differently between question text and response options.
In Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Are Information Retrieval Approaches Good at Harmonising Longitudinal Survey Questions in Social Science?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the sub-concept in the response categories of the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard</span> question could fundamentally alter the concept being measured.
Conversely, <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">qualified</span> questions do not capture any sub-concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While information retrieval (IR) techniques have advanced significantly in recent years, their applicability to survey harmonisation remains under explored. Traditional approaches like BM25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson et al., 2009</a>)</cite> excel at lexical matching but may miss semantic equivalences obscured by terminology shifts. Contemporary neural models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpukhin et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">MacAvaney et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab and Zaharia, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al., 2022</a>)</cite> while powerful at semantic modelling risk overfitting to superficial patterns without capturing the domain-specific nature of survey concepts. Moreover, existing work rarely addresses the unique requirements of structured survey questions where both question text and coded response options jointly define the measured construct. In this collaboration between computer scientists and survey specialists,
we bridge this gap through three main contributions:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate the task of detecting conceptual equivalence in longitudinal surveys as an IR problem, specifically addressing the challenge of integrating question and response text as coherent input sequences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a systematic evaluation of unsupervised IR approaches on a dataset spanning 75 years (1946-2020) by comparing probabilistic models (BM25), linearly probed language models (DeBERTa, Qwen), and IR-specialised neural networks (M3-Embedding).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We combine quantitative metrics based on topic code alignment with qualitative analysis by domain experts to reveal both the promise and limitations of current IR methods for survey harmonisation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our results reveal that while IR-optimised neural models achieve the highest overall performance (<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1=0.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.79</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1=0.79</annotation></semantics></math>), traditional approaches like BM25 remain competitive (<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1=0.75</annotation></semantics></math>). Notably, qualitative analysis reveals that all models struggle with questions showing high lexical overlap but divergent sub-concepts, suggesting the need for more sophisticated approaches to concept granularity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Computational Social Science</h3>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Survey data harmonisation is the process of making different surveys more comparable and consistent across various variables such as studies, concepts, and time.
Existing approaches rely heavily on classification schemas that are manually annotated by experts. These methods are typically statistical or procedural based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubrow and Tomescu-Dubrow, 2016</a>)</cite>, thus leading to challenges (e.g. language barriers) when conducting cross-cultural research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
On the other hand, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stöhr, 2024</a>)</cite> proposed to train domain-specific Word2Vec embeddings using social science literature. Their evaluation of various tasks such as text classification with data collected from a sociology dictionary showed promising results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Information Retrieval Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional IR continuous to adopt probabilistic-based methods that are based on statistical evidence (e.g. term-frequencies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson et al., 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salton and Buckley, 1988</a>)</cite>).
Recently, neural IR models have garnered increasing attention by performing searches based on the underlying semantic information. Pre-trained language models like DeBERTa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2021</a>)</cite> are commonly adopted as the reader model to embed sequences for a downstream retrieval model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">MacAvaney et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab and Zaharia, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Some works also proposed IR-specialised models with various training strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and architectural designs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that aim at getting the best of both worlds (i.e. syntactic and semantic features).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose there are <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sets of questionnaires <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S={\{S_{1},S_{2},...,S_{n}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S={\{S_{1},S_{2},...,S_{n}\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}{S_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}{S_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of all questionnaires.
For each query question and the corresponding response options <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,r)\in{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,r)\in{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective of the experiment is to identify all potential <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">conceptually equivalent</span> in <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U-\{(q,r)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U-\{(q,r)\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset</h3>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use questions from a large-scale longitudinal social science survey project consisting of 318 questionnaires. The questions possess diverse longitudinal spans ranging from 1946 to 2020. In total, there are 42,161 questions with three different typologies: <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard, qualified</span>, and <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compound</span> questions, as illustrated in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Are Information Retrieval Approaches Good at Harmonising Longitudinal Survey Questions in Social Science?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard</span> questions have the most diverse distribution of concepts by presenting concepts and sub-concepts in the questions and response options, respectively.
This introduces an inherent complexity and additional challenge since <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard</span> questions only appear as code list questions.
As such, we consider code list questions<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            30,863 code list questions in total.</span></span></span> only and construct our input sequence as the concatenation of the question and response options.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following experiments, all models are <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">training-free</span>.
Our baseline model is BM25<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            We use the bm25s library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lù, 2024</a>)</cite> for efficient implementations</span></span></span>, a probabilistic model which retrieves equivalent items based on term frequencies. We employ linear probing on pre-trained LLMs as reader models to embed input sequences. Following the bi-encoder paradigm, inputs (i.e. questions) are encoded independently.
Various encoder models including the SBERT<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            The chosen base model is <span id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">multi-qa-mpnet-base-dot-v1</span>.</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite> sentence transformer, DeBERTa-v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and pre-trained decoder model like Qwen<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            We use Qwen-2.5 with 3B parameters and 4-bit quantisation.</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> are considered.
For efficiency, we follow the late interaction framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab and Zaharia, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al., 2022</a>)</cite> where representations are pre-computed offline with cosine distance (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-cosine\_similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-cosine\_similarity</annotation></semantics></math>) as the similarity measure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we investigate different ways of generating sequence representations in the DeBERTa and Qwen models.
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Mean-pooled sequence representation (mean-rep).</span> The mean of all the token representations from the final encoder or decoder layer is taken.
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Sequence summary token (SST-rep).</span> For encoder models, we use the [CLS] representation. For decoder models, we use the last token representation due to the autoregressive nature of decoder models.
The chosen SBERT model uses [CLS] pooling for sequence representation.
Since the model is trained end-to-end using [CLS] pooling, we do not investigate the impact of using mean-rep as this will result in sub-optimal performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the IR-specialised model, we utilise M3-Embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a multi-lingual, multi-granularity, and multi-functionality embedding model designed for IR tasks. It is trained with self<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>knowledge distillation to harmonise dense, sparse (lexical), and multi-vector retrieval signals within a single architecture. The backbone model is a pre-trained XLM-RoBERTa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conneau et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. During retrieval, it uses mean-rep and SST-rep to capture both word ordering and context nuances.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we compare two variants of M3-Embedding, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BGE-m3</span> and <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BGE-reranker-m3</span>. The former follows the bi-encoder paradigm while the latter follows the cross-encoder paradigm where query questions and the corresponding candidate question are encoded in pairs. <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BGE-m3</span> is leveraged for both end-to-end ranking and re-ranking task while <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BGE-reranker-m3</span> is used for the re-ranking task only.
In both models, the score function is the weighted<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            We follow the hyper-parameter settings as suggested in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</span></span></span> sum of the three retrieval scores mentioned above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, for the re-ranking task, neural models are asked to refine the top-50 questions per query from BM25. Other than the <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BGE-reranker-m3</span>, all other models follows the LLM linear probing setup as described above.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Survey questions are tagged using a hierarchical label ontology with 16 top-level topics (e.g. Education, Health) and 120 fine-grained sub-topics (e.g. Education-Primary Schooling, Education-Higher Education).
We evaluate model performance by comparing the top-level topic codes between the query question and its most equivalent match as identified by the model. This is based on the assumption that equivalent questions address the same general topic.
Note that there is no randomness in the experiments as our models are <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">training-free</span> (i.e. possess fixed weights).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Results</h2>

